<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louisiana Purchase</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1803">1803</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> Purchase was the acquisition by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s territory of Louisiana in 1803 for about 15 million dollars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> had returned the colony to France shortly before, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> sold it after failing to hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>. The purchase roughly doubled the size of the United States, though its vague boundaries left disputes such as that over West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase.jpg" alt="Louisiana Purchase" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Morris · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louisiana_Purchase.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> from 1682 until it ceded the territory to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the secret <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fontainebleau-1762">Treaty of Fontainebleau</a> of 1762. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the main issue was free transit down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>. Pinckney's Treaty of 27 October 1795 gave American merchants the right of deposit at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, but Spain revoked it in 1798 and restored it only in 1801. Meanwhile, in 1800, Spain had ceded Louisiana back to France in the secret <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-treaty-of-san-ildefonso">Third Treaty of San Ildefonso</a>, in exchange for territories in Tuscany. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> sent General Charles Leclerc to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> in December 1801, American fears grew that French troops would then go to New Orleans.</p><p>President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>, eager to gain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, sent Robert R. Livingston to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1801 and later tasked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-monroe">James Monroe</a> and Livingston with buying the city. Pierre Samuel du Pont de Nemours helped by back-channel diplomacy and introduced the idea of a much larger purchase. The failed French effort to suppress the revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> and the prospect of renewed war with the United Kingdom led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> to consider selling. Negotiating with the French treasury minister François Barbé-Marbois, the American envoys quickly agreed to buy the whole territory once it was offered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The price was 80 million francs, about 15 million dollars, down from an initial 100 million francs. Of this, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> assumed up to 20 million francs of French debts owed to its citizens and paid 60 million francs in government bonds at 6 per cent interest, which the banking houses of Baring and Hope bought. The land nominally comprised 828,000 square miles, most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>'s drainage basin west of the river. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> controlled only a small part of it, and most was inhabited by Native Americans, so the United States chiefly bought the pre-emptive right to obtain their lands by treaty or conquest. The non-native population was about 60,000, half of them enslaved Africans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Jefferson considered a constitutional amendment but was persuaded otherwise by his cabinet. Federalists opposed the purchase over its cost and constitutionality, and feared the power of the new western states and of new slave-holding states; some Northern Federalists explored a separate northern confederacy. A House vote to deny the request failed by 59 to 57. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> was handed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> on 20 December 1803, three weeks after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> transferred the colony to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the territory was organised in 1804. Its boundaries were not defined and were disputed with Spain until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adams-onis-treaty">Adams–Onís Treaty</a> of 1819, and its northern edge was adjusted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1818.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louisiana Purchase” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Thomas Jefferson looked forward to a time when the nation’s “rapid multiplication” would “cover the whole northern, if not the southern continent”, and the <b>Louisiana Purchase</b> of 1803 set the stage for continental expansion.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/manifest-destiny">Manifest destiny · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>After Louisiana entered the Union as a state, the Territory of Louisiana, formed in 1805 from the part of the <b>Louisiana Purchase</b> north of the 33rd parallel, was renamed the Missouri Territory.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-4">4 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It completed the transfer of the <b>Louisiana Purchase</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-10">10 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Missouri was to enter the Union as a slave state and Maine as a free one, with slavery barred from the rest of the <b>Louisiana Purchase</b> north of 36°30'.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-6">6 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The strip of Gulf Coast between the Mississippi and Perdido rivers remained Spanish after the <b>Louisiana Purchase</b>, though the United States claimed it.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-west-florida">Republic of West Florida</a></li><li><blockquote>Spanish officials still governed until 1803, when the colony was handed to France and, weeks later, to the United States under the <b>Louisiana Purchase</b>, doubling American territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-louisiana">Spanish Louisiana</a></li></ul></section>
